            <ArticleTitle>[Electrophysiological studies of chronic pelvic and perineal pain].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>905-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.purol.2010.08.061</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To describe electrophysiological studies, what they investigate, and their contribution and limitations in the assessment of pelvic and perineal pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Description of the electrophysiological techniques generally used to evaluate somatic nerves of the pelvic and perineal region (analytical electromyography, nerve conduction velocities, reflexology), their applications and the difficulties of interpretation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Electrophysiological studies can demonstrate signs in favour of peripheral neuropathy, specify the axonal and/or demyelinating type of lesion, and provide topographic arguments on the type of trunk or nerve root involved (pudendal nerve, sacral nerve roots, ilioinguinal or iliohypogastric nerve, genitofemoral nerve, obturator nerve).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Electrophysiological studies require a technically skilled operator and can provide a better understanding of some types of pain, but are not sufficiently sensitive and specific. The conclusions of electrophysiological study reports must be closely correlated with clinical findings.</AbstractText>
